1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,325
A machine learning model makes predictions using adjustable
numbers called parameters.

2
00:00:06,812 --> 00:00:11,487
Training means finding parameter values that reduce prediction
error.

3
00:00:11,975 --> 00:00:17,100
To see how this works, imagine a model with just one
parameter, called w.

4
00:00:17,588 --> 00:00:20,962
Each possible value has a corresponding loss.

5
00:00:21,450 --> 00:00:24,400
Plot those losses, and we get a landscape.

6
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:30,150
In this simplified example, the loss is w minus three,
squared.

7
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:33,050
The lowest point occurs at three.

8
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:36,800
We can see that because the whole curve is visible.

9
00:00:37,450 --> 00:00:42,325
But a training algorithm needs a rule it can apply from its
current position.

10
00:00:42,975 --> 00:00:45,750
What information tells it which way to move?

11
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:53,125
The slope gives us that information.

12
00:00:53,775 --> 00:00:59,600
Here, the curve slopes downward as w increases, so moving
right reduces the loss.

13
00:01:00,250 --> 00:01:04,250
On the other side, the slope is positive, so moving left
helps.

14
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:07,250
At the bottom, the slope is zero.

15
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:10,275
The derivative measures this slope.

16
00:01:10,925 --> 00:01:16,300
With many parameters, the corresponding collection of
derivatives is called the gradient.

17
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:23,875
Gradient descent moves opposite the gradient.

18
00:01:24,525 --> 00:01:30,375
Take the current parameter, subtract the gradient multiplied
by a learning rate, and repeat.

19
00:01:31,025 --> 00:01:34,325
Starting at minus one, our slope is minus eight.

20
00:01:34,975 --> 00:01:40,000
With a learning rate of zero point two, the next parameter is
zero point six.

21
00:01:40,650 --> 00:01:44,850
Subtracting a negative slope moves us right, toward lower
loss.

22
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:54,675
Repeat the same calculation, and the point approaches the
bottom.

23
00:01:55,238 --> 00:01:58,362
The learning rate controls the size of each step.

24
00:01:58,925 --> 00:02:01,900
A small rate makes steady but slow progress.

25
00:02:02,462 --> 00:02:04,988
A suitable rate gets there faster.

26
00:02:05,550 --> 00:02:10,225
A rate that is too large can jump across the valley and move
farther away.

27
00:02:10,788 --> 00:02:14,262
For this curve, positive rates below one converge.

28
00:02:14,825 --> 00:02:17,050
At one, the point oscillates.

29
00:02:17,612 --> 00:02:20,138
Above one, it moves farther away.

30
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:24,400
A downhill direction does not guarantee a good step.

31
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:28,850
Real models usually have many parameters.

32
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:33,350
We can picture two of them as positions on a contour map.

33
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:40,650
The gradient points toward the steepest local increase; its
negative points toward the steepest local decrease.

34
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:49,000
Complex landscapes may contain several valleys, so gradient
descent does not guarantee the best possible solution.

35
00:02:49,650 --> 00:02:55,275
But the working rule stays the same: measure the slope, take a
controlled step, and repeat.
